s a standard for each of those which  is based on what is the healthy level  for those epa has six what we call  criteria pollutants they're basic air  pollutants for which we regulate  nationally we set an air quality  standard for each of the six glutens and  as the standards are currently set most  parts of the country meet all the  standards and there are a few standards  which some areas struggle to achieve a  zone is one and that's probably the one  for DC  is the most challenging ozone is  actually one of the the most problematic  of the of the pollutants in this area  and it's mainly from the car exhaust  those oxygens from the NOx and the and  the Sox kind of hit each other combined  and do it there's a photochemical  reaction that takes place that creates  ozone at the ground level what we call  ground-level ozone so where as the upper  level ozone is actually good to protect  us the ground-level ozone is as harmful  to EPA as established standards for six  of these criteria pollutants and for a  host of what we call it air toxic  pollutants air toxics or hazardous air  for you the number one public health  killer is fine particulate matter  particulate matter is a mixture of solid  particles and liquid droplets found in  the air it is formed in the atmosphere  as a result of chemical reactions  between pollutants such as sulfur  dioxide and nitrogen oxide which are  emitted from cars industries and power  plants some particles are big enough to  be seen with the naked eye others are so  small that they can only be seen with an  electron microscope in the 1980's EPA  let analysis based on emerging science  which which which reveal that  the particulates that were of most  concern from a health standpoint were  very fine particulates particles that  were much smaller than we have been  focusing on my particulate matter is a  big problem because the whole story with  fine particulate matter is that the  particles are fine they're small and  they get breathe 